        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Jack Kerouac', passage='Jack Kerouac wrote "On the Road" on a continuous 120-foot scroll of paper in just three weeks. He taped together sheets of tracing paper to avoid interrupting his flow by changing pages. The novel was rejected by publishers for six years before being accepted. Kerouac coined the term "Beat Generation" and was a central figure in the movement. He died at age 47 from complications related to alcoholism.', entity='120-foot scroll in three weeks', question='How did Jack Kerouac write "On the Road"?', answer='on a continuous 120-foot scroll of paper in three weeks', explanation='Jack Kerouac wrote "On the Road" on a continuous 120-foot scroll of paper in just three weeks to maintain his writing flow.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Toni Morrison', passage='Toni Morrison was the first African American woman to win the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1993. She didn\'t publish her first novel until she was 39 years old. Morrison worked as an editor at Random House, where she helped publish works by other Black authors. Her novel "Beloved" won the Pulitzer Prize and was inspired by the true story of Margaret Garner, an escaped slave.', entity='first African American woman Nobel', question='What Nobel Prize distinction does Toni Morrison hold?', answer='first African American woman to win Nobel Prize in Literature', explanation='Toni Morrison was the first African American woman to win the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1993.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Ray Bradbury', passage='Ray Bradbury wrote "Fahrenheit 451" on a rented typewriter in the basement of UCLA\'s library, paying 10 cents per half hour. The novel took him nine days to write and cost $9.80 in typewriter rental fees. Bradbury never learned to drive a car and didn\'t fly in an airplane until he was 62. He wrote every day for 70 years and published over 30 books.', entity='rented typewriter 10 cents per half hour', question='How did Ray Bradbury write "Fahrenheit 451"?', answer='on a rented typewriter paying 10 cents per half hour', explanation='Ray Bradbury wrote "Fahrenheit 451" on a rented typewriter in UCLA\'s library basement, paying 10 cents per half hour.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Kurt Vonnegut', passage='Kurt Vonnegut was a prisoner of war during World War II and survived the bombing of Dresden, which inspired his novel "Slaughterhouse-Five." He worked as a journalist and in public relations before becoming a full-time writer. Vonnegut attempted suicide in 1984 but survived. His novels often feature the phrase "So it goes" and dark humor about war and death.', entity='survived bombing of Dresden', question='What World War II experience inspired Vonnegut\'s "Slaughterhouse-Five"?', answer='surviving the bombing of Dresden as a POW', explanation='Kurt Vonnegut was a prisoner of war who survived the bombing of Dresden, which inspired his novel "Slaughterhouse-Five."'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Sylvia Plath', passage='Sylvia Plath was the first person to win a posthumous Pulitzer Prize for Poetry in 1982. She met her husband, poet Ted Hughes, at a party where she bit his cheek and drew blood. Plath suffered from depression and made several suicide attempts before succeeding at age 30. Her novel "The Bell Jar" was initially published under the pseudonym Victoria Lucas.', entity='first posthumous Pulitzer Poetry', question='What Pulitzer Prize distinction does Sylvia Plath hold?', answer='first person to win posthumous Pulitzer Prize for Poetry', explanation='Sylvia Plath was the first person to win a posthumous Pulitzer Prize for Poetry in 1982.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Harper Lee', passage='Harper Lee published only two novels in her lifetime: "To Kill a Mockingbird" and "Go Set a Watchman." She was childhood friends with Truman Capote and helped him research "In Cold Blood." Lee was extremely reclusive and rarely gave interviews. "To Kill a Mockingbird" won the Pulitzer Prize in 1961 and has sold over 40 million copies worldwide.', entity='childhood friends with Capote', question='What famous author was Harper Lee childhood friends with?', answer='Truman Capote', explanation='Harper Lee was childhood friends with Truman Capote and helped him research his novel "In Cold Blood."'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Truman Capote', passage='Truman Capote\'s "In Cold Blood" is considered the first non-fiction novel. He spent six years researching and writing the book, interviewing the killers extensively. Capote had an eidetic memory and never took notes during interviews. He was known for his flamboyant personality and high-pitched voice. He died at age 59 from complications related to alcoholism and drug abuse.', entity='first non-fiction novel', question='What literary innovation is "In Cold Blood" credited with?', answer='first non-fiction novel', explanation='Truman Capote\'s "In Cold Blood" is considered the first non-fiction novel, blending journalism with literary techniques.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Zora Neale Hurston', passage='Zora Neale Hurston was an anthropologist as well as a writer, studying African American folklore in the South. She was the most published Black woman writer of her time but died in poverty and obscurity. Her novel "Their Eyes Were Watching God" was rediscovered in the 1970s and is now considered a classic. Hurston was known for her vibrant personality and refusal to portray Black people as victims.', entity='died in poverty and obscurity', question='What tragic end did Zora Neale Hurston face despite her literary achievements?', answer='died in poverty and obscurity', explanation='Despite being the most published Black woman writer of her time, Zora Neale Hurston died in poverty and obscurity.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='James Joyce', passage='James Joyce\'s "Ulysses" was banned in many countries for obscenity and wasn\'t legally available in the United States until 1933. The novel takes place in a single day (June 16, 1904) in Dublin. Joyce was nearly blind for much of his writing career due to glaucoma and other eye problems. He spoke 17 languages and incorporated multiple languages into his writing.', entity='spoke 17 languages', question='How many languages did James Joyce speak?', answer='17', explanation='James Joyce spoke 17 languages and incorporated multiple languages into his complex literary works.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Marcel Proust', passage='Marcel Proust\'s "In Search of Lost Time" is one of the longest novels ever written, with over 1.5 million words. He wrote much of it while bedridden with asthma, working at night and sleeping during the day. Proust lined his bedroom with cork to block out noise. The novel was initially rejected by publishers, including André Gide, who later called it his greatest regret.', entity='over 1.5 million words', question='How long is Marcel Proust\'s "In Search of Lost Time"?', answer='over 1.5 million words', explanation='Marcel Proust\'s "In Search of Lost Time" is one of the longest novels ever written, with over 1.5 million words.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Franz Kafka', passage='Franz Kafka asked his friend Max Brod to burn all his unpublished works after his death, but Brod refused and published them instead. Kafka worked as an insurance investigator by day and wrote at night. He was a vegetarian and health enthusiast who practiced nudism. Most of his major works were published posthumously, including "The Trial" and "The Castle."', entity='asked friend to burn works', question='What did Franz Kafka ask his friend to do with his unpublished works?', answer='burn them after his death', explanation='Franz Kafka asked his friend Max Brod to burn all his unpublished works after his death, but Brod refused and published them instead.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Flannery O\'Connor', passage='Flannery O\'Connor was diagnosed with lupus at age 25 and died at 39, but wrote some of the most powerful short stories in American literature. She raised peacocks on her family farm in Georgia and incorporated Southern Gothic elements into her writing. O\'Connor was a devout Catholic, and religious themes permeate her work. She wrote only two novels but over 30 short stories.', entity='raised peacocks', question='What unusual animals did Flannery O\'Connor raise on her farm?', answer='peacocks', explanation='Flannery O\'Connor raised peacocks on her family farm in Georgia, which influenced her Southern Gothic writing.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Langston Hughes', passage='Langston Hughes was one of the leading figures of the Harlem Renaissance. He wrote his first published poem, "The Negro Speaks of Rivers," when he was just 19 years old. Hughes traveled extensively, working as a seaman, cook, and busboy while writing. He was one of the first writers to focus on the lives of working-class African Americans. His ashes are interred beneath a floor medallion in the Schomburg Center in Harlem.', entity='first poem at 19', question='How old was Langston Hughes when he wrote his first published poem?', answer='19', explanation='Langston Hughes wrote his first published poem, "The Negro Speaks of Rivers," when he was just 19 years old.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Tennessee Williams', passage='Tennessee Williams\' real name was Thomas Lanier Williams III. He suffered a nervous breakdown in his 30s and spent time in a mental institution. His plays "A Streetcar Named Desire" and "Cat on a Hot Tin Roof" both won Pulitzer Prizes. Williams was openly gay at a time when it was dangerous to be so. He died by choking on a bottle cap in a New York hotel room.', entity='died choking on bottle cap', question='How did Tennessee Williams die?', answer='choking on a bottle cap', explanation='Tennessee Williams died by choking on a bottle cap in a New York hotel room, a tragic and unusual end.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Arthur Miller', passage='Arthur Miller was married to Marilyn Monroe from 1956 to 1961. His play "The Crucible" was written as an allegory for McCarthyism and the Communist witch hunts of the 1950s. Miller was called before the House Un-American Activities Committee and refused to name names of suspected Communists. He won the Pulitzer Prize for "Death of a Salesman" in 1949.', entity='married to Marilyn Monroe', question='Which famous actress was Arthur Miller married to?', answer='Marilyn Monroe', explanation='Arthur Miller was married to Marilyn Monroe from 1956 to 1961, one of the most famous literary-Hollywood unions.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='William Faulkner', passage='William Faulkner created the fictional Yoknapatawpha County, Mississippi, setting for many of his novels. He was rejected by the U.S. Air Force in World War I for being too short (5\'5") and too light (125 pounds). Faulkner won two Pulitzer Prizes for Fiction and the Nobel Prize in Literature. He was known for his stream-of-consciousness writing style and complex, multi-generational narratives.', entity='rejected for being too short', question='Why was William Faulkner rejected by the U.S. Air Force?', answer='too short and too light', explanation='William Faulkner was rejected by the U.S. Air Force in World War I for being too short (5\'5") and too light (125 pounds).'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='John Steinbeck', passage='John Steinbeck won both the Pulitzer Prize and Nobel Prize in Literature. His novel "The Grapes of Wrath" was banned and burned in many places for its political content. Steinbeck worked as a laborer, ranch hand, and journalist before becoming a successful writer. He traveled across America with his dog Charley in a camper truck, which inspired his book "Travels with Charley."', entity='banned and burned', question='What happened to "The Grapes of Wrath" due to its political content?', answer='banned and burned in many places', explanation='John Steinbeck\'s "The Grapes of Wrath" was banned and burned in many places for its political content about migrant workers.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Edith Wharton', passage='Edith Wharton was the first woman to win the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1921 for "The Age of Innocence." She designed her own homes and gardens and wrote a book on interior decorating. Wharton lived in France for much of her later life and helped refugees during World War I. She was nominated for the Nobel Prize in Literature three times but never won.', entity='first woman Pulitzer Fiction', question='What Pulitzer Prize distinction does Edith Wharton hold?', answer='first woman to win Pulitzer Prize for Fiction', explanation='Edith Wharton was the first woman to win the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1921 for "The Age of Innocence."'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Willa Cather', passage='Willa Cather grew up on the Nebraska frontier, which inspired many of her novels about pioneer life. She was one of the first professional female journalists in the United States. Cather lived with her partner Edith Lewis for 39 years in what was likely a romantic relationship. She won the Pulitzer Prize for "One of Ours" in 1923. Cather often dressed in men\'s clothing and went by the name "William" as a young person.', entity='lived with partner 39 years', question='How long did Willa Cather live with her partner Edith Lewis?', answer='39 years', explanation='Willa Cather lived with her partner Edith Lewis for 39 years in what was likely a romantic relationship.'),
        
        QAPair(topic='literature', example='Saul Bellow', passage='Saul Bellow won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1976 and three National Book Awards. He was born in Canada but moved to Chicago as a child, which became the setting for many of his novels. Bellow was married five times and had children with four different women. He continued writing into his 80s and published his last novel at age 85. His novel "The Adventures of Augie March" begins with one of the most famous opening lines in literature.', entity='married five times', question='How many times was Saul Bellow married?', answer='five times', explanation='Saul Bellow was married five times and had children with four different women throughout his life.'),
